2026-04-22 | pattern-watch — possessive-attribution-scope | triggered by claim-white-1985-069 | possessive-form attribution ("Aristotle's analysis", "his psychology") differs structurally from prepositional-scope (079: "For the Greeks") and verbal-attribution (127: "Aristotle characterizes") in that the possessor is grammatical owner of the claim's subject, integrating the attribution more tightly with the claim's content. Registered separately from the attribution-scope-not-referent watch (079) to preserve the option of treating these as related-but-distinct patterns at codification. Re-evaluation at item-25 alongside the 079 watch; jointly evaluate whether to (a) merge into single attribution-scope pattern with documented sub-forms, (b) codify as separate AF/PL codes, or (c) keep the 079 watch broad and possessive as a parked sub-pattern. Current observations: 1 (this case). Trigger: ≥2 more possessive-form cases by item-25, OR joint evaluation triggered by 079 watch firing first.

2026-04-22 | observation — observational-vs-preventive-pattern-instances | triggered by 069 attribution-scope evaluation | distinction between "observational" instances (the pattern's structure is present in the claim but no candidate label was up for consideration) and "preventive" instances (the pattern's labeling rule actually prevented an error — a candidate label was rejected on the rule's basis). 079 is preventive (Greeks/Greek/ancients thin-slug cluster was a candidate; rejected on attribution-scope basis); 127 and 069 are observational (Aristotle was not in either item's `_candidate_metadata.key_concepts`). §7.1 promotion threshold ("≥3 instances") doesn't currently distinguish; consider whether promotion thresholds should weight preventive instances differently, since they validate the rule whereas observational instances only confirm pattern presence. For item-25 evaluation. Affects: attribution-scope-not-referent watch count clarification — 079 reg + 127 obs = 2 instances logged but only 1 preventive; same clarification applies to the new possessive-attribution-scope watch (069 = 1 observational, 0 preventive so far).

2026-04-22 | observation — s32-distortion-criterion-edge-case | triggered by claim-white-1985-069 (psychology vs investigation of the soul) | §3.2 paraphrase-distortion criterion ("labels-from-quote vs labels-from-paraphrase differ → distortion") would fire on this item if Soul/psychology were labeled, but the difference is scholarly-synonym (psychology ≈ peri psychēs in Aristotelian context), not concept-shift. Item-25 candidate: §3.2 row may need a "scholarly-synonym carve-out" sub-clause excluding established translation-pairs from the distortion criterion. Not blocking this item's annotation under Option 1 minimal labels (neither term labeled — Imagination only).

2026-04-22 | observation — single-thin-slug-attestation | triggered by claim-white-1985-069 | psychology slug observed in candidate set but not labeled (Option 1 minimal labels). Single-instance attestation logged in case future items surface psychology as a label and the slug-quality issue (Caston-introduced thin-only def with no aliases or cross-linking) becomes actionable. Not slug-graveyard (single slug, not cluster — slug-graveyard pattern requires multiple thin sibling slugs for the same concept, per 007 arithmetic cluster and 033 doxa cluster registrations). Cheap log against retrospective need.

2026-04-22 | observation | triggered by claim-ogorman-2005-100 | §5.4 partial-faithfulness verification second activation (first was 066). Procedure executed as designed: looked up source record, verified each label grounded in quote, ruled distortion vs elaboration. Finding: paraphrase added "not presently perceived" inference for the compared-to objects (extractor's faithfulness_note explicitly flags this); inference is interpretive narrowing (absent-objects qualifier) rather than new-referent introduction — labels-from-quote and labels-from-paraphrase coincide as [Imagination, appearance]. Ruled elaboration not distortion per §3.2 criterion; no PL:paraphrase-distortion flag. Two-data-point sample for §5.4 calibration: both activations to date (066 quote-truncation, 100 inference-narrowing) ruled elaboration. Item-25 candidate: assess whether §5.4 has surfaced any genuine distortion case yet.

2026-04-22 | observation — multi-english-thin-slug-tiebreaker | triggered by claim-ogorman-2005-100 (sizing up / comparative seeing / comparison) | structurally novel sub-case of §3.2 ontology-synonymy: three thin English slugs from three different authors (O'Gorman, Frede, Heidegger respectively) for the same concept (mental comparison). §3.2 decided rule "English form preferred" does NOT disambiguate when all candidates are English — tiebreaker rule is missing. Resolution on this case: cognitive-operation aspect not labeled at all (not load-bearing + O'Gorman's own key_concepts cite both `sizing up` AND `comparative seeing`, so claim's-own-author heuristic doesn't uniquely pick) — sidesteps the tiebreaker question rather than resolving it. **Pre-specified item-25 question:** when a future case forces a pick among multiple English thin slugs, which heuristic wins? Candidate rules to evaluate: (A) surface-closest-to-claim-text, (B) claim's-own-author preference, (C) most-canonical/most-cited term, (D) ad-hoc per case with documented rationale. **Explicit trigger:** first divergence case where labeling is required AND heuristics A/B/C disagree — forces tiebreaker-rule formalization regardless of item count. At n=1; do not register pattern-watch yet — but logged so item-25 has the actionable question pre-specified rather than re-derived.

2026-04-22 | observation — slug-graveyard-vs-cross-author-synonymy | triggered by 100 | slug-graveyard requires within-author/within-domain thin-slug proliferation for *related* concepts (007 arithmetic cluster, 033 doxa cluster). 100's cognitive-operation cluster (sizing up / comparative seeing / comparison) is cross-author thin-slug proliferation for the *same* concept — structurally inverse. Counted toward multi-english-thin-slug-tiebreaker pattern (entry 2), not slug-graveyard. Slug-graveyard count stays at 1 of 2 needed.

2026-04-22 | observation — load-bearing-modifier-applied-to-functional-characterization | triggered by 049 (motivating in "potentially motivating content") + 036 (visible in "not yet visible") + 053 (actual in "actual sensation") + 138 (persist in "persist independently") | the load-bearing-modifier rule applies when a modifier specifies the claim's thesis (removing it changes what the claim asserts). Distinguished from predicate work (item 100's "sizing up") where the modifier describes what the operative concept does. Diagnostic: does removing the modifier change the claim's thesis (load-bearing → label) or only describe-what-it-does (predicate work → drop). [UPDATED 2026-04-22 with 138 case: fourth precedent satisfies the pre-specified "consider promotion to §3 sub-rule at item-25 if a fourth case extends the pattern" condition. Item-25 codification LOCKED on agenda; the rule has accumulated four diverse cases (motivating, visible, actual, persist) across multiple syntactic positions (claim-content modifier on object, predicate-modifier on subject-position, adverbial-modifier on the predicate verb) — variation sufficient to attempt formalization rather than wait for additional cases.] Four precedents; codification-consideration locked for item-25.

2026-04-22 | pattern-watch — prepositional-attribution-scope-individual-author | triggered by 049 ("for Aristotle") | individual-author prepositional attribution-scope (parallel to 079's "For the Greeks" collective-prepositional). Registered as a separate sub-pattern from the 079 collective-prepositional watch, and from the 069 possessive-attribution sub-pattern. The three syntactic forms (collective-prepositional, individual-prepositional, possessive-on-subject) are anticipated by 079's general framing but differ in tightness of integration with claim content. Joint evaluation at item-25 alongside 079 and 069: (a) merge into single attribution-scope-not-referent pattern with documented sub-forms, (b) codify as separate AF/PL codes per syntactic form, (c) keep 079 broad and treat the others as parked sub-patterns. Current preventive observation count: 0 (this case is observational — Aristotle wasn't a candidate). Trigger: ≥1 preventive case OR joint evaluation at item-25.

2026-04-22 | observation — sub-type-labeling-precedent | triggered by 049 (sensitive phantasia for "Sensory phantasia") | first instance of labeling a phantasia sub-type rather than the umbrella Imagination canonical. Rule applied: when claim_text names a sub-type and ontology has umbrella-rich + sub-type-thin nodes for the same concept-cluster, label the sub-type per §3.0 (a) concept-match-at-correct-level-of-abstraction; the umbrella is wrong-abstraction (broader than what the claim deploys). Don't double-label umbrella + sub-type. Future cases: deliberative phantasia (likely), proper-sensibles vs common-sensibles, sensitive vs intellective faculty distinctions. Pattern preserved for retrospective reference; not pattern-watch (single rule application, not a pattern with internal variation). Item-25 candidate: §3.0 (a) corollary explicitly addressing umbrella/sub-type relation.

