2026-04-22 | observation | triggered by claim-nussbaum-1985-049 | new corpus-data condition: source record's `quote` field is the empty string but the `ground` field carries substantive textual basis for the claim ("The animal who 'phantasizes' perceives an object as a thing of a certain sort that could become an object of pursuit or avoidance"). The `ground` field is doing the work `quote` normally does in §5.4 verification; appears to be a paraphrastic-summary slot that fills in when the extractor couldn't get a clean verbatim. Latent issue — 049 is supported faithfulness, so §5.4 procedure didn't fire; would have failed lookup if 049 were partial. Trace observation; no §5.4 edit needed at this item. Parallels 036 (empty nearby_provenance for aporetic claim) and 091 (Aristotelian Bekker number on Heidegger source) as data-quality anomalies; see source-record-schema-variability-cluster entry.

2026-04-22 | observation — source-record-schema-variability-cluster | triggered by 049 | three accumulated data-quality observations: 036 (empty nearby_provenance for methodological aporetic), 091 (Aristotelian Bekker number on Heidegger source), 049 (empty quote with ground field doing the work). Pattern: source record schema is not uniform across genres or claim-types. §5.4 partial-faithfulness procedure currently assumes specific fields populated; will produce null returns or tool failures on schema-variant items. None has surfaced as a §5.4 blocker yet (036 supported, 091 supported, 049 supported), but all three would be active issues if their items had partial faithfulness. Item-25 candidate: §5.4 extension explicitly handling schema variability — case-by-case fallbacks (ground field when quote empty, citation field when nearby_provenance null, etc.).

2026-04-22 | observation — extractor-truncation-blind-spot-in-s54 | triggered by 138 (Frede passage review) | the §5.4 partial-faithfulness procedure compares source-quote-field vs claim_text but does not compare source-quote-field vs the larger argumentative passage from which the quote was extracted. On 138, the full Frede passage articulates phantasiai as "motions in the soul caused by sense-perceptions" immediately before "sensory images or imprints that can exist independently from their original source." The extractor's quote captures only the latter; claim_text matches the truncated quote. §5.4 verifies as elaboration (no distortion at the quote-vs-paraphrase comparison level), but the *full-passage-vs-quote* comparison would surface a missed referent (Movement/Change). Item-25 candidate: §5.4 extension addressing extractor-level truncation, possibly via a "passage-context check" sub-step when the source record permits access to surrounding text. Latent issue — only consequential when the truncated material includes referents that would change the label set, as here.

2026-04-22 | observation — slug-graveyard-frede-cluster | triggered by 138 | Frede phantasiai cluster (phantasiai, phantasma, phantasmata, sensory images, imprints, persistence, after-image — ~7+ Frede-introduced thin slugs with no aliases or cross-linking) has surface shape of slug-graveyard pattern (within-author within-domain thin-slug proliferation, parallel to 007 Aristotelian arithmetic and 033 Aristotelian doxa clusters). Would be observation #2 of slug-graveyard pattern-watch (1 of 2 → 2 of 2 → §7.1 trigger fires). Deferred: passage review reveals Frede's terminological practice is substantively rich (distinguishes phantasiai from informed-by-thought representations, articulates the causal account from sense-perception to phantasiai-as-images, etc.); the cluster of thin slugs in the ontology under-represents the conceptual care Frede actually deploys. This is an ontology-curation observation more than a §7.1 slug-graveyard preventive-instance question. Promotion-relevant question is "does ontology proliferate thin slugs without curation?" — answered yes by 007/033/138 — but the policy response is ontology-curation work, not annotation-flag promotion. Trigger considered NOT FIRED on slug-graveyard pattern-watch; count stays at 1 of 2. Item-25 candidate: re-evaluate whether slug-graveyard pattern's promotion path is the right tool for what the pattern is actually surfacing (ontology-curation gaps).

2026-04-22 | observation — multi-thin-slug-synonymy-intra-author | triggered by 138 (Frede: phantasiai = sensory images = imprints) | when a single author's claim asserts identity between multiple of their own thin slugs, label by surface-prominence (subject position of claim_text). Distinct from 100's cross-author case: there, the synonymy is implicit-via-ontology (multiple authors' terms collide on the same concept); here, the synonymy is explicit-via-claim (the author asserts it). On 138, surface-prominence converges with technical-Greek-term tiebreaker (phantasiai is both subject position and Greek transliteration). Future divergence cases (subject position is English thin slug, alternative is Greek transliteration also thin) will force a sub-tiebreaker decision. Parking-lot until divergence; pattern-watch only if divergence appears.

2026-04-22 | observation — passage-review-changes-analysis-procedural-pattern | triggered by 138 (Movement/Change blind-spot revealed) + 036 (visible→Disclosedness upgrade) + 049 (motivation inclusion confirmed) | three items where post-proposal passage review substantively changed the analysis. On 036, passage forced upgrade I had resisted (visible → Disclosedness on hermeneutic-disclosure evidence). On 049, passage confirmed inclusion I had resisted (motivation as load-bearing thesis-modifier). On 138, passage surfaced a §5.4 blind spot I would have missed entirely (Movement/Change as truncated referent). Pattern: surface-fidelity-discipline is roughly correct as default but systematically blind to what full passage reveals — three different failure modes (under-upgrade, over-conservative, structural-blind-spot). Procedural implication: passage review should be a routine check rather than triggered-on-confidence; the user's discipline of running it forced the corrections. Item-25 candidate: consider whether annotation procedure should formalize passage-review as a step (paralleling §5.4 partial-faithfulness procedure) for cases where the analysis depends on a single phrase's surface reading.
